> University project (with Rammal Farah) on multimodal image/text retrieval, built entirely from scratch.

# Introduction

## Context

The assignment was to work with a small dataset of images spread across 4 classes, each paired with text captions, and progressively build up to a CLIP-style model able to search images by caption and captions by image — without relying on a pretrained CLIP checkpoint.

## The problem

Before jumping to CLIP, we first tried the "obvious" approaches: a CNN classifier for the images and a small transformer classifier for the captions. Both plateaued well below a usable accuracy, which is what actually motivated moving to a shared image/text embedding space instead of two separate classifiers.

# My Solution

## First attempt: a CNN from scratch

We built and trained a CNN image classifier for the 4 classes, inspired by a standard Keras image-classification architecture, with dropout layers to fight overfitting on such a small dataset. Debugging the training curves surfaced a real architectural bug: a bottleneck that collapsed 200,704 neurons down to just 64 between two layers, destroying most of the spatial information. Even after fixing the architecture, accuracy plateaued around 50% — the "from scratch" CNN approach had hit its ceiling.

## Second attempt: a text classifier with smallBERT

In parallel, we trained a caption classifier (smallBERT-based) to assign one of the 4 classes to a sentence (e.g. "pepper the aussi pup" → "dog"). Validation accuracy stagnated around 82%, with a growing train/validation gap from epoch 4-5 onward — a clear overfitting signal, likely fixable with a lighter architecture and L2 regularization, but caption classification wasn't the core goal of the project, so we moved on.

## Building CLIP from scratch

Both classifiers being capped, we switched to a proper CLIP architecture: a custom image encoder (built on top of a ResNet50 backbone, without its classification head) and a custom text encoder, each projecting into a shared latent space of reduced dimensionality. The two encoders are trained jointly with a contrastive objective so that an image and its matching caption end up close together in that space, on properly separated train/validation/test splits.

## Retrieval by cosine similarity

At inference time, every image and every caption in the gallery is encoded once into that latent space. A query (an image or a caption) is embedded the same way, and the best matches are found by computing the dot product of the normalized query embedding against every gallery embedding (`q @ embeds.T`) and sorting the resulting similarity scores — since the embeddings are normalized, that dot product is exactly the cosine similarity.

# Conclusion

Going through the CNN and smallBERT dead ends first turned out to be the most useful part: it made the case for CLIP's shared embedding space very concrete, instead of just being "the next architecture to try". The final model supports both directions of retrieval — image → caption and caption → image — on the 4-class dataset, and gave us a hands-on understanding of contrastive multimodal training that a pretrained checkpoint would have skipped entirely.
